@h1 Glossary & cross-references h1@

Define your terms, figures, and sections @strong once strong@ as data, reference
them by id, and render a glossary or figure list from the same source. As with
every self-organising view, editing the data updates every place that reads it.

@h2 A glossary from data h2@

Model the glossary as a collection of records — each with a term and its gloss —
and iterate it into a definition list:

@@@pre
#glossary: [
  { term - Attention, gloss - A moral faculty of focus. },
  { term - Fresnel, gloss - A stepped lens for lighthouses. }
]

@dl
(each @glossary as g) @dt @g.term dt@ @dd @g.gloss dd@ (end)
dl@
pre@@@

That renders a real @code <dl> code@ with a term and description per entry
(verified). Read each field with data access — @@@code @g.term code@@@ — not a
capture hole.

@h2 Cross-references and a shared bibliography h2@

Give each referenced thing a unique @code id code@ field and read it wherever you
cite it. For a bibliography that assembles across chapter files, combine data
with @a |href /docs/guides/additive-partials/| additive partials a@: each
chapter contributes with @@@code +#bibliography code@@@, and the master renders
the merged node. Keep the reference call shape uniform so every citation reads
the same way.

@h2 What is not built in h2@

@strong Automatic numbering strong@ ("Figure 3") and @strong back-links strong@
("see above") are @em not em@ core features. Either carry the numbers in your
data, or compute them in a @@@code <% %> code@@@ script (see
@a |href /docs/guides/derived-content/| Derived content a@). The core language
gives you the single source of truth and the views; the numbering layer is a
renderer or script concern.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Mixing @@@code ::term:: code@@@ and @@@code @g.term code@@@ for the same read
— use data access @@@code @g.term code@@@ outside a definition body. li@
@li Assuming numbering and back-links are automatic — they are not. li@
ul@

